rns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the core mechanism right. It cites IRC §852(b)(6) nonrecognition and explains that in-kind delivery of low-basis lots removes embedded gains from the shared portfolio, which can shrink capital gains distributions for all classes, mutual fund holders included. It also correctly says cash-based trading can still realize gains, that mutual fund cash redemptions force gain-realizing sales, and that the benefit depends on redemption volume and lot selection. However, it has substantive omissions. (1) It never says that remaining securities get no basis step-up, so embedded gains are removed rather than eliminated. (2) It never says the fund-level nonrecognition rule does not exempt redeeming investors or APs from their own gains. (3) It frames mutual fund cash flows only as offsetting the benefit. It does not say they can impose realized gains and transaction costs on ETF-class holders. (4) It ties the benefit only to volume and lots, not to strategy or practical in-kind capacity. Minor imprecisions: 'pro rata by NAV' sharing is a simplification, and the Rule 18f-3 point about allocating basket costs is tangential. The central mechanism is correct, but two key-point limits are missing.\"}","ttft_ms":3308,"type":"result","duration_ms":7217,"uuid":"7eeb89e4-4c30-4de8-8e42-5317e591aec5","ttft_stream_ms":536,"time_to_request_ms":61,"first_content_frame_ms":536,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
